r policy  reform and why former presidential  candidate Mitt Romney was out on a March  hosted by an openly left-wing campaign  group the right and left divide has  broken down these past few weeks  driven by America's collective memory of  its history of racism what England  remembers is the Victorian era Americans  remember as a time of slavery and the  Civil War Jim Crow was in living  as our parents and grandparents join  Martin Luther King jr. to march on  Washington it's fresh and Ross still in  a way that is unique to the nation the  protests in Britain as in America have  been used as cover for those with an  agenda of violence and destruction but  most people marching will have done so  in sympathy with America and to  highlight issues that also exist in the  UK and there's one key difference in  both countries we have seen videos of  people throwing themselves between  protesters and police but in the UK  protesters have been stepping in to  protect the police from stones and glass  bottles to this American it was a very  moving sight that emphasized the  differences between policing and my  homeland and my adopted country America  is policed by force while the UK is  policed by consent something worth  holding on to that was Kate Andrews and  that's all for this week he thanks very  much for listening and thanks to our  producer agus Carter  The Spectator magazine combines incisive  political commentary with books and arts  reviews of unrivaled Authority stock up  on your summer reading with a 12 week  subscription in print and online for  just pile pounds plus we'll send you a  copy of Spectator columnist Lionel  Shrivers new book the motion of the body  through space absolutely free go to  spectator coudl UK forward slash Lionel

