 into the harbor the statue of a man  whose trade was notorious for throwing  six slaves with no monetary value into  the sea 1890s Britain raised that statue  1890s Britain the decade in which my  grandparents were children for heaven's  sake had only just closed the slave  market in Zanzibar and if you want to  know more about that stain on our fairly  recent history read Aleister Hazel's  enthralling horrifying the last slave  market  dr. John Kirk and the struggle to end  the East African slave trade if we're  entitled to feel pride at the  achievements and virtues of our  forebears why should we not feel shame  at their sins  if there is significance in erecting  monuments to those we admire who can  deny the significance of tearing down  monuments to those we don't be honest  the sculpting of a massive  representation of Sir Edward Colston for  public display was not an exercise in  either history or biography it was a  salutation plain and simple and if 1890s  Britain was free to raise a monument to  a man whom many of the residents of  Bristol wished to salute 2020s Britain  should be free to raise two fingers to a  man whose memory their successors hate  toppling that statue was not an attempt  to erase history as disgruntled  conservatives liked to mutter of history  the moving finger writes and having writ  moves on nor all thy piety nor wit shall  Lear it back to cancel hath align nor  all thy tears wash out a word of it no  that statue was not about history but  estimation our era is entitled to its  own response far from this being a  writing out of history it was a writing  in of history history is what happened  how dreary then to recommend that  instead of letting people show that  passion unbidden and unmerited the  statue should instead have been demoted  by a deliberative committee of municipal  were these followed by the sending of a  van with council workers in high-vis  jackets and the dispatch of the bronze  to some dull and unvisited corner of a  museum somewhere Sir Edward went out not  with a whimper but with a bang and the  video of that statue going into the  harbor is now already in the modern  Museum that is the internet that day has  become itself history history made not  by an item on the agenda for councillors  and bureaucrats but by the impulse of an  angry crowd watching the video scene of  the mob toppling the bronze Sir Edward  over the key side last week I felt a  kind of thrill at the beating heart of  human beings responding viscerally to a  piece of art the statue of course was no  more than an item of fairly  unexceptional art it served as proxy as  heroic sculpture does for its subject in  this case Sir Edward but then serving as  proxy for the things that may represent  is what a great deal of art is all about  just as it is good when art lifts  inspires in Nobles and pleases us it's  good to when art infuriates hurts or in  flames  good in both cases because art has  engaged as something in me resonates at  people's rage against a material object  serving as proxy for what they hate so I  also thrilled to people's rage against  art itself art they do not like for this  to is a function of art to engage in  November 1954 on his 80th birthday  Winston Churchill was formally presented  with his portrait by one of our most  notable 20th century artists sir Graham  Sutherland the painting in my view a  masterpiece is of a figure at the same  time peevish and heroic obdurate and  defiant  fat bull-necked and irascible with a  hint of hurt in the eyes the portrait is  completely and infuriatingly him his  political enemies tended to think it was  rather good his friends mostly took the  other view Churchill detested it said it  made him look like a drunk propped up  from the gutter accepted this gift of  both houses of parliament with what  grace he could muster not a lot  took it to Chartwell and sent it down  into the basement after a lady Spenser  Churchill's death it emerged that she  had had it removed and burnt I call that  act of aggression against art a triumph  for art art should delight wound or  enrage  lady Spencer Churchill's vandalism was  the ultimate tribute to honest  portraiture I see the whole episode as a  celebration of the power of figurative  representation to arouse passion in the  breasts of the viewer and passion will  take its natural course  Sutherlands art had cut his subject to  the quick Sutherland was entitled to his  artistic response to Churchill and the  Churchill's were entitled to their  response to him this is where art should  be not on elaborate plinths or the walls  of hushed galleries but at the center of  attention and human passion whether the  passion is sympathetic or antipathetic  his secondary heroic sculpture is  multidisciplinary hovering between art  craftsmanship and historical tribute  Colston's bronze like Sutherlands oils  is at commentary to see these  commentaries engulfed in the flames of a  wife's anger or drowned by the Bristol  Ian's in Bristol Harbor is to see art  come out of the galleries and text books  and tourist snapshots and into our  hearts and spleens thus perished most of  the innumerable monuments to General  Franco that I remember from parks and  squares in pre-1970 Spain thus came down  the statue of Saddam Hussein whose  toppling in Baghdad was so far as I  remember unaccompanied by much regret  from we who are of a conservative habit  of mind and thus fell a slave traders  monument in the destruction of art's  handiwork art shows it matters comes  alive that's was Matthew Paris and  finally Kate's Andrews America can often  look to outsiders like a country of two  warring tribes the Trump --is-- anti PC  Brigade and the woke Twitterati such  divisions certainly exists our  broadcasters our party political and  partisanship is deeply entrenched in  America's two-party system it's tempting  to see the scenes in recent weeks as the  continuation of tribal warfare by  there means but the truth is far more  complicated America has the most  militarized and aggressive police force  in the Western world the country's  legacy of rapid expansion combined with  vast geography and open landscapes  engendered a sense of lawlessness early  on and need to be protected from it this  has led to an implicit social contract  police need to be armed to match the  criminals they're dealing with it's the  price for keeping the country safe  when the police bought vehicles that  look like tanks or dressed as if they  were off to war we didn't ask questions  in theory you knew it must go wrong  sometimes but what did going wrong look  like who did it effect it was hard to  imagine no longer the ubiquity of  smartphones means misdeeds are now  filmed and shared instantly we see  America's police officers treat  low-level offenders and even innocent  citizens with the same force and  aggression you would expect to see used  against the most violent criminals black  Americans feel the overwhelming brunt of  the brutality when the system goes wrong  they suffer the most it's a common trap  - plans that cherry-pick statistics and  say nothing to see black Americans are  no more likely to be shot by police than  they were five years ago most who were  shot were carrying guns 14 of the 55  unarmed Americans shot by police last  year were black but despite accounting  for 13% of the population African  Americans make up more than a quarter of  the thousand fatal police shootings that  take place each year an unarmed black  man is four times more likely to be  fatally shot than his white counterpart  over the past two decades nearly 30,000  Americans have been registered dead at  the hands of police causes of death  include tasers strongholds and head  injury there is now growing awareness of  these tactics and it's forcing us to ask  are we okay with this  George Floyd was killed by a cops knee  shoved into his neck for nearly ten  minutes he died by asphyxiation not a  gunshot so his death wouldn't be  registered on the fatal shootings list  but it registered with the nation  showing how out of hand police brutality  is in America it fits  trend earlier in lockdown a mod Arbor II  a 25 year old black man was shot dead by  a former police man and his son while  out jogging in Georgia then there was  Elijah Tufano 14 punched and roughed up  by a California cop for possession of a  cigar and Floyd is not the first time  we've heard I can't breathe on camera  Eric Gardner uttered the same word six  years ago when the police put him in a  chokehold and strangled him to death for  selling single cigarettes on the street  after protests Floyd's killer was  charged so too were the cops who looked  on their action and inaction exposes a  culture one that has been allowed to  exist because not enough Americans  thought it worth confronting their  action and inaction exposes a culture  one that has been allowed to exist  because not enough Americans thought it  worth confronting and of course far-left  protesters have joined the marches but  to focus on them is fundamentally to  misunderstand what's happening it's not  about the murder of one man but the  countless black men Floyd's death  represents and the Warped social  contract that has allowed it to happen  the justice reforms of the 1980s and  1990s have had the unintended effect of  worsening race relations Bill Clinton's  war on drugs using prison as its main  tool disproportionately puts black  Americans behind bars drugs are used and  sold equally by black and white  Americans but black Americans are six  times more likely to be incarcerated for  a drug offense meanwhile the Supreme  Court's establishment of qualified  immunity for the police allows officers  to only face punishment if their crime  clearly infringes on an established  often technical right this has  encouraged appalling excesses that's why  we find former presidents George W Bush  and Barack Obama calling for policy  reform and why former presidential  candidate Mitt Romney was out on a March  hosted by an openly left-wing campaign  group the right and left divide has  broken down these past few weeks  driven by America's collective memory of  its history of racism what England  remembers is the Victorian era Americans  remember as a time of slavery and the  Civil War Jim Crow was in living  as our parents and grandparents join  Martin Luther King jr. to march on  Washington it's fresh and Ross still in  a way that is unique to the nation the  protests in Britain as in America have  been used as cover for those with an  agenda of violence and destruction but  most people marching will have done so  in sympathy with America and to  highlight issues that also exist in the  UK and there's one key difference in  both countries we have seen videos of  people throwing themselves between  protesters and police but in the UK  protesters have been stepping in to  protect the police from stones and glass  bottles to this American it was a very  moving sight that emphasized the  differences between policing and my  homeland and my adopted country America  is policed by force while the UK is  policed by consent something worth  holding on to that was Kate Andrews and  that's all for this week he thanks very  much for listening and thanks to our  producer agus Carter  The Spectator magazine combines incisive  political commentary with books and arts  reviews of unrivaled Authority stock up  on your summer reading with a 12 week  subscription in print and online for  just pile pounds plus we'll send you a  copy of Spectator columnist Lionel  Shrivers new book the motion of the body  through space absolutely free go to  spectator coudl UK forward slash Lionel
 

